doc_id: architectural-patterns
title: Architectural Patterns
project: ai-workflow
version_hint: "2024-2025"
kind: layered_architecture_guide
description: Defines the strict layered architecture ensuring separation of concerns and testability across routers, services, repositories, and contracts/DTOs.
scope: Establishes the high-level architectural layers, their responsibilities, allowed call directions, and typical request lifecycle; detailed patterns for each layer are in separate documents.
primary_runtime_references[5]:
  - path: app/routes/example_router.py
    role: Router layer implementation example.
  - path: app/services/example_service.py
    role: Service layer implementation example.
  - path: app/repositories/example_repository.py
    role: Repository layer implementation example.
  - path: app/contracts/example_contract.py
    role: Contract/DTO layer implementation example.
  - path: app/core/factory.py
    role: Application factory wiring middleware, routers, and lifespan.
transitional_note: References to .md files in related_pattern_docs are transitional and will be updated to .toon filenames once those documents are converted in later phases.
related_pattern_docs[6]:
  - router-patterns.toon
  - service-patterns.toon
  - repository-patterns.toon
  - api-patterns.toon
  - exception-patterns.md
  - middleware-patterns.md
sections[7]:
  - id: layer-overview
    index: 1
    title: Layer overview and call directions
    category: architecture
    summary: The architecture enforces strict unidirectional dependencies to ensure testability and separation of concerns.
    items[7]:
      - id: overview.layers
        type: rule
        text: "The application is organized into four primary layers: Routers (HTTP), Services (business logic), Repositories (data access), and Contracts/DTOs (data structures)."
      - id: overview.call-direction
        type: rule
        text: "Allowed call direction is strictly downward: Routers -> Services -> Repositories. Upper layers may depend on lower layers, but never the reverse."
      - id: overview.contracts-shared
        type: rule
        text: Contracts/DTOs are shared across all layers for type safety; they define the data shapes passed between layers.
      - id: overview.no-skip-layers
        type: rule
        text: Routers must not call repositories directly; all data access must go through services to maintain business logic centralization.
      - id: overview.http-boundary
        type: rule
        text: Only routers handle HTTP concerns (status codes, headers, request/response objects); services and repositories are HTTP-agnostic.
      - id: overview.di-wiring
        type: rule
        text: Dependencies flow via constructor injection; the application factory in app/core/factory.py wires infrastructure (pools, wrappers) into repositories and services.
      - id: overview.testability
        type: rule
        text: Each layer can be tested in isolation by mocking the layer below; this is the primary benefit of strict layer separation.
  - id: request-lifecycle
    index: 2
    title: Typical request lifecycle
    category: architecture
    summary: Describes the flow of a typical HTTP request through all architectural layers.
    items[8]:
      - id: lifecycle.step1
        type: note
        text: "1. Request arrives at FastAPI; middleware (logging, security headers, error handling) processes it first."
      - id: lifecycle.step2
        type: note
        text: "2. FastAPI matches the route and invokes the router handler with validated path/query/body parameters."
      - id: lifecycle.step3
        type: note
        text: "3. Router handler uses Depends() to obtain service instances (constructed with injected repositories)."
      - id: lifecycle.step4
        type: note
        text: "4. Router calls service method(s), passing validated contract DTOs."
      - id: lifecycle.step5
        type: note
        text: "5. Service orchestrates business logic, calling one or more repositories for data access."
      - id: lifecycle.step6
        type: note
        text: "6. Repositories execute queries against SurrealDB, Elasticsearch, or other stores and return domain models."
      - id: lifecycle.step7
        type: note
        text: "7. Service returns result to router; router returns Pydantic response model to FastAPI."
      - id: lifecycle.step8
        type: note
        text: "8. FastAPI serializes response; middleware processes outgoing response (compression, headers)."
  - id: routers-layer
    index: 3
    title: Routers layer
    category: layer_definition
    summary: Handle HTTP request/response lifecycle using APIRouter instances registered in app/core/factory.py. See app/routes/example_router.py for a reference implementation.
    items[6]:
      - id: routers.role
        type: rule
        text: Handle HTTP request/response lifecycle exclusively; routers are the entry point for HTTP concerns.
      - id: routers.pattern
        type: rule
        text: Use APIRouter instances registered in app/core/factory.py; never attach routes directly to the app object.
      - id: routers.no-business-logic
        type: rule
        text: No business logic in routers; delegate all processing to the service layer.
      - id: routers.response-model
        type: rule
        text: Use Pydantic models for response_model and include summary, description, and responses for OpenAPI documentation.
      - id: routers.error-handling
        type: rule
        text: Let services raise domain exceptions; global exception handlers (registered in factory) translate them to HTTP responses.
      - id: routers.dependencies
        type: rule
        text: Use FastAPI Depends() with Annotated type aliases to inject services; routers should not construct services directly.
  - id: services-layer
    index: 4
    title: Services layer
    category: layer_definition
    summary: Encapsulate business logic with class-based services and dependency injection. See app/services/example_service.py for a reference implementation.
    items[7]:
      - id: services.role
        type: rule
        text: Encapsulate all business logic; services are the heart of the application.
      - id: services.pattern
        type: rule
        text: Implement services as classes with constructor-based dependency injection.
      - id: services.lifecycle.init
        type: rule
        text: Use __init__ to initialize dependencies explicitly via constructor parameters.
      - id: services.lifecycle.process
        type: rule
        text: Implement core logic in named methods representing business actions (verbs like create, process, calculate).
      - id: services.lifecycle.shutdown
        type: rule
        text: Provide an optional shutdown() method for cleanup of resources when needed.
      - id: services.orchestration
        type: rule
        text: Services may call multiple repositories and external clients; they own transaction boundaries and coordinate units of work.
      - id: services.domain-errors
        type: rule
        text: Raise domain-specific exceptions (from app/core/errors.py) rather than HTTP exceptions; see exception-patterns.md for the error hierarchy.
  - id: repositories-layer
    index: 5
    title: Repositories layer
    category: layer_definition
    summary: Provide storage-agnostic data access abstraction over SurrealDB, Elasticsearch, and future stores. See app/repositories/example_repository.py for a reference implementation.
    items[6]:
      - id: repositories.role
        type: rule
        text: Encapsulate all direct database and search index interactions; hide storage details behind stable Python interfaces.
      - id: repositories.pattern
        type: rule
        text: Define repository protocols (typing.Protocol) and implement concrete classes that accept connection pools via constructor.
      - id: repositories.domain-mapping
        type: rule
        text: Return domain models or DTOs (Pydantic models from app/contracts/), not raw database rows or driver-specific structures.
      - id: repositories.no-transactions
        type: rule
        text: Repositories must not commit or roll back transactions; transaction boundaries are owned by services.
      - id: repositories.http-agnostic
        type: rule
        text: Repositories never import FastAPI or raise HTTP exceptions; they are unaware of the transport layer.
      - id: repositories.testability
        type: rule
        text: Repository protocols enable easy substitution with fakes or mocks for service unit testing.
  - id: contracts-layer
    index: 6
    title: Contracts and DTOs layer
    category: layer_definition
    summary: Define data structures and validation rules using Pydantic models with strict configuration. See app/contracts/example_contract.py for a reference implementation.
    items[5]:
      - id: contracts.role
        type: rule
        text: Define data structures and validation rules for request/response payloads and internal data transfer.
      - id: contracts.pattern
        type: rule
        text: Use Pydantic models with strict configuration (ConfigDict) for all contracts.
      - id: contracts.extra-forbid
        type: rule
        text: "Always set ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\") to reject unknown fields in JSON payloads."
      - id: contracts.validation
        type: rule
        text: Use Annotated and Field for validation constraints; define custom validators (@model_validator) for complex logic.
      - id: contracts.shared
        type: rule
        text: Contracts are shared across layers; routers use them for request/response, services for internal transfer, repositories for domain models.
  - id: cross-cutting-concerns
    index: 7
    title: Cross-cutting concerns
    category: architecture
    summary: Middleware and exception handling span all layers and are configured in the application factory.
    items[5]:
      - id: crosscutting.middleware
        type: rule
        text: Middleware (logging, security headers, compression) is registered in app/core/factory.py and processes requests before routers and responses after serialization. See middleware-patterns.md.
      - id: crosscutting.exception-handlers
        type: rule
        text: Global exception handlers translate domain errors to HTTP responses; they are registered in the application factory and documented in exception-patterns.md.
      - id: crosscutting.lifespan
        type: rule
        text: Application lifespan events (startup/shutdown) initialize and clean up infrastructure resources (connection pools, search clients) in app/core/factory.py.
      - id: crosscutting.settings
        type: rule
        text: Configuration flows from a single Settings instance created at startup; it is injected into services and infrastructure via dependency injection.
      - id: crosscutting.health-checks
        type: rule
        text: Health endpoints (/health for liveness, /api/v1/health for readiness) are registered separately from domain routers and documented in api-patterns.toon.
